/* data/learn-c/phy-12-08.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-08.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-08"] = {"8.1":{"s":"This chapter goes beyond the electric field of Class 11. It uses Gauss' law for symmetric charges. Then it covers electric potential and potential energy, conductors and dielectrics, and capacitors and their energy. It ends with the Van de Graaff generator.","f":[{"x":"phi = q/epsilon_0","n":"Gauss' law: total electric flux out of any closed surface; q = net charge inside that surface (air or vacuum).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.1, p.186"}],"w":["Only the charge INSIDE the closed surface counts for the flux; charges outside change the field but not the total flux."]},"8.2":{"s":"Gauss' law makes the field easy to find when the charge is symmetric. Outside a charged sphere the field is as if all charge sat at the centre: E = q/(4πε₀r²). Inside a charged conducting shell the field is zero. Near a large flat sheet with charge density σ, E = σ/(2ε₀), the same at every distance.","f":[{"x":"E = q/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r^2)","n":"Field of a uniformly charged spherical shell at a point outside it (r > R), as if all charge q sat at the centre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.187"},{"x":"E = sigma/epsilon_0","n":"Field just at the surface of the charged shell (r = R); sigma = charge per unit area.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.187"},{"x":"E = lambda/(2*pi*epsilon_0*r)","n":"Field at distance r from a very long straight wire; lambda = charge per unit length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.187"},{"x":"E = sigma/(2*epsilon_0)","n":"Field near a large flat sheet of charge; the same at every distance, pointing away from the sheet if sigma is positive.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.2, p.188"}],"w":["Inside a uniformly charged shell E = 0, but the potential is not zero: it equals the value at the surface.","Sheet of charge: σ/(2ε₀). Just outside a charged conductor: σ/ε₀. Do not mix them up."]},"8.3":{"s":"Electric potential at a point is the work done per unit positive charge to bring it from infinity to that point, measured in volt (J/C). Potential difference between two points is the work per unit charge to move a charge between them. The field points from high to low potential, and E is the rate at which potential falls with distance.","f":[{"x":"U = q_1*q_2/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r)","n":"Potential energy of two point charges r apart (zero at infinite distance).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.189"},{"x":"V = U/q","n":"Potential = potential energy per unit charge; unit volt = joule per coulomb.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.190"},{"x":"E = -dV/dx","n":"The field is the negative potential gradient: it points the way the potential falls fastest.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.190"},{"x":"1 eV = 1.6*10^-19 J","n":"Electron volt: energy gained by an electron through 1 V.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.3, p.189"}],"w":["Potential is a scalar (add with signs); field is a vector (add as vectors).","The minus sign in E = -dV/dx: the field points from high to low potential."]},"8.4":{"s":"For a point charge, V = q/(4πε₀r). Potential is a scalar, so for many charges you simply add the potentials, with signs. For a dipole, V = p cos θ/(4πε₀r²): it falls faster with distance than for a single charge and is zero on the equatorial line.","f":[{"x":"V = q/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r)","n":"Potential at distance r from a point charge q (zero at infinity); positive for +q, negative for -q.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.4, p.191"},{"x":"V = p*cos(theta)/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r^2)","n":"Potential of a short dipole (r much larger than its length); theta measured from the dipole axis, p = q*2l.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.4, p.193"},{"x":"V = (1/(4*pi*epsilon_0))*(q_1/r_1 + q_2/r_2 + ... + q_n/r_n)","n":"Superposition: potentials of several charges simply add, with their signs.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.4, p.193"}],"w":["On the equatorial line of a dipole V = 0, but E is not zero there.","A dipole's potential falls as 1/r²; a single charge's falls as 1/r."]},"8.5":{"s":"An equipotential surface has the same potential everywhere on it. No work is needed to move a charge along it. Field lines always cross equipotential surfaces at right angles. For a point charge they are spheres centred on the charge; for a uniform field they are parallel planes.","w":["Two equipotential surfaces never cross: that would give two field directions at one point.","No work is done moving a charge along an equipotential surface, however long the path."]},"8.6":{"s":"The potential energy of two point charges is U = q₁q₂/(4πε₀r): positive for like charges, negative for unlike ones. A dipole in a uniform field has U = −pE cos θ. It is lowest (stable) when the dipole lines up with the field.","f":[{"x":"U = q_1*q_2/(4*pi*epsilon_0*r_12)","n":"Energy stored in assembling two point charges from infinity; for many charges add this over every pair.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.196"},{"x":"PE = q*V(r)","n":"Potential energy of a charge q placed where the external potential is V(r).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.197"},{"x":"tau = p*E*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a dipole in a uniform field E; theta = angle between p and E.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.198"},{"x":"U(theta) = -p*E*cos(theta)","n":"Potential energy of a dipole in a uniform field (zero chosen at theta = 90 degrees).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.6, p.199"}],"w":["Put the signs of the charges into U = q₁q₂/(4πε₀r): a negative U means the pair attracts.","Dipole: torque is largest at 90 degrees; energy is lowest at 0 degrees (stable) and highest at 180 degrees (unstable)."]},"8.7":{"s":"Conductors have free electrons that can move through the material; insulators do not. In a conductor at rest, the field inside is zero, any extra charge sits on the outer surface, and the surface is an equipotential. In insulators, charges stay bound to their atoms.","w":["Extra charge on a conductor sits on its outer surface; the field inside the conducting material is zero even when the conductor is charged."]},"8.8":{"s":"A dielectric is an insulator that can be polarised. In a field, its positive and negative charges shift slightly, so the molecules act as tiny dipoles. Polar molecules already have dipoles, which line up; non-polar ones get induced dipoles. This reduces the field inside the material by a factor k, the dielectric constant.","f":[{"x":"P = chi_e*E","n":"Polarisation (dipole moment per unit volume) of a linear dielectric; chi_e = electric susceptibility (0 for vacuum).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.8, p.202"}],"w":["A dielectric lowers the field inside it by the factor k; it does not change the free charge on the plates of an isolated capacitor."]},"8.9":{"s":"A capacitor stores charge and energy. Its capacitance C is the charge it holds per unit potential difference: C = Q/V, in farads. In a series combination every capacitor carries the same charge and the voltages add, so the reciprocals of the capacitances add, and the result is smaller than the smallest one. In a parallel combination every capacitor has the same voltage and the charges add, so the capacitances add.","f":[{"x":"C = Q/V","n":"Capacitance; unit farad (1 F = 1 coulomb per volt). 1 microfarad = 10^-6 F, 1 picofarad = 10^-12 F.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.203"},{"x":"1/C_eq = 1/C_1 + 1/C_2 + ... + 1/C_n","n":"Capacitors in series.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.205"},{"x":"C_eq = C_1 + C_2 + ... + C_n","n":"Capacitors in parallel.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.205"},{"x":"C_eq = C/n (series), C_eq = n*C (parallel)","n":"n equal capacitors of capacitance C each.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.9, p.205"}],"w":["In a series combination the capacitor with the smallest capacitance has the largest voltage across it.","Series: the reciprocals add; parallel: the capacitances add. It is the opposite of resistors."]},"8.10":{"s":"A parallel plate capacitor with plate area A and gap d has capacitance C = ε₀A/d. Filling the gap with a dielectric of constant k multiplies the capacitance by k. A slab that fills only part of the gap increases C by a smaller amount.","f":[{"x":"E = sigma/epsilon_0","n":"Field between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor (zero outside); sigma = Q/A.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.206"},{"x":"C = epsilon_0*A/d","n":"Parallel plate capacitor with air or vacuum between plates of area A, distance d apart.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.207"},{"x":"C = epsilon_0*A/(d - t + t/k)","n":"With a dielectric slab of thickness t (less than d) and dielectric constant k between the plates.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.208"},{"x":"C = k*C_0","n":"Dielectric filling the whole gap: capacitance rises k times.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.10, p.208"}],"w":["Use A in square metres and d in metres; 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m².","A metal slab (k very large) of thickness t gives C = ε₀A/(d − t)."]},"8.11":{"s":"No electrons cross the gap of a charging capacitor, yet the current in the circuit continues. Maxwell explained this with displacement current: a changing electric field in the gap acts like a current and also produces a magnetic field. It equals ε₀ times the rate of change of electric flux.","f":[{"x":"i_d = epsilon_0*A*(dE/dt)","n":"Displacement current between the plates (vacuum or air); it equals the conduction current i_c in the wires.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.11, p.209"}],"w":["Displacement current flows only while E is changing; for a steady field it is zero."]},"8.12":{"s":"Charging a capacitor needs work, which is stored as electric energy in the field between the plates. Energy stored U = Q²/(2C) = ½ C V² = ½ Q V.","f":[{"x":"U = Q^2/(2*C) = (1/2)*C*V^2 = (1/2)*Q*V","n":"Energy stored in a capacitor with charge Q at potential difference V; Q = C*V.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 8.12, p.210"}],"w":["Battery still connected: V stays fixed. Battery removed: Q stays fixed. Choose the form of U that keeps the fixed quantity.","While charging, the battery gives QV but only QV/2 is stored; the rest is lost as heat in the wires."]},"8.13":{"s":"The Van de Graaff generator builds up a very high voltage, of the order of millions of volts. A moving insulating belt carries charge to a large hollow metal sphere. Because charge on a conductor moves to its outer surface, charge keeps collecting there. It is used to accelerate charged particles for nuclear physics.","w":["It works because charge given to a hollow conductor moves to its outer surface, so charge keeps collecting on the dome."]}};
